      "evidenceText": "Trump Veers Back Into Claims of Rigged Election, Questioning Midterm Results - The New York Times Advertisement SKIP ADVERTISEMENT You have a preview view of this article while we are checking your access. When we have confirmed access, the full article content will load. For years, President Trump has claimed without evidence that any election he lost or even feared losing was rigged. So it was a surprise to many when, on election night after his party was routed in the midterms last week, he said he was “absolutely willing” to accept the results . That lasted less than a week. “Does anybody really believe that this ‘Election’ was honest,” Mr. Trump wrote on Monday on social media, renewing his efforts to sow doubt about the outcome in the face of staggering Democratic wins. “How could the Polls be so wrong??? Some early voting ‘dumps’ were so large that it was impossible to win, no matter how many people went to vote. It’s all about the machines, which are far more important than the voter!” Mr. Trump has in recent days returned to his penchant for amplifying conspiracy theories about widespread election fraud. On Saturday, he had shared several posts from others questioning the results with convoluted mathematical theories about election rigging. Election officials have said the vote went reasonably smoothly , with few major issues reported and no evidence of widespread fraud. Mr. Trump had tried to lay the groundwork for claims of fraud before Election Day, mounting an intense campaign to assert more federal control over the election process and sow doubt about the outcome. Much of his campaign was blocked by the courts. His administration directed federal agents to scour state rolls and federal databases to try to find noncitizen voters. The administration sued 30 states and the District of Columbia for refusing to cede to the federal government’s demands to turn over voter rolls. But his claims of large numbers of noncitizens illegally voting fell apart in court, and the administration lost more than two dozen of those cases. Thank you for your patience while we verify access. If you are in Reader mode please exit and log into your Times account, or subscribe for all of The Times. Thank you for your patience while we verify access. Already a subscriber? Log in . Want all of The Times? Subscribe . Related Content Advertisement SKIP ADVERTISEMENT",
