      "id": "ae746c85ae93ecb604efa518f40bce8c2e04ac0a0ff43c010234e5f6334ff213",
      "title": "682: quantum of sollazzo",
      "url": "https://knowwhereconsulting.co.uk/blog/one-hundred-famous-views-of-edo",
      "category": "uncategorized",
      "checkedAt": "2026-11-16T19:21:28.847Z",
      "httpStatus": 200,
      "finalUrl": "https://knowwhereconsulting.co.uk/blog/one-hundred-famous-views-of-edo/",
      "contentType": "text/html; charset=UTF-8",
      "pageTitle": "One Hundred Famous Views of Edo",
      "pageDescription": "This map was inspired by a post from Doug Greenfield about Hiroshige&#039;s woodblock prints. I wrote a short prompt and Claude did the rest with almost no input. Between 1856 and his death in 1858, Utagawa Hiroshige made over a hundred woodblock prints of Edo, the city that became Tokyo. The series was arranged by season. It shows bridges, temples, riverbanks, festivals and Mount Fuji on the horizon, often framed through a bold foreground of branches, boats or bodies. Van Gogh copied two of them. This map puts all 119 prints at the spot each view was drawn from, on a map of the modern city.",
      "evidenceText": "One Hundred Famous Views of Edo - KnowWhere Skip to content Doug Greenfield posted his Map of the Week – Map of the 100 Famous Views of Edo saying “The Wikipedia listing has a table listing each print with an image, title and geographic coordinates. Somebody with more time and better programming skills could make a nice interactive map from this table with popups at each location.” Well I have the time and an AI with some programming skills so I thought “Why not?” So I asked Claude: One Hundred Famous Views of Edo Have a look at the table on https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/One_Hundred_Famous_Views_of_Edo . It has a link to each of the woodblock images and coordinates. Can you make an interactive web map of these locations and images in the style of Hiroshige woodblocks. Use my standard architecture, tools and place in a project folder named “edo” Tell me what else you need from me to start Claude asked me a couple of questions, suggested a way to style the map and pop-ups and suggested that it add a season selector following the classification of the Hiroshige woodblocks and also including the Japanese text from the wiki page to add an authentic feel. I gave it the go ahead and left it whirring while I jumped on a call. An hour later I had this map which needed a slight tweak to the pop-ups and I decided to add an inset map to give users a sense of where Edo (Tokyo) is in Japan, this isn’t visible in the mobile version. Claude gave me a summary of what it had done: All 119 prints. I wrote a script, extract.py, that reads Wikipedia’s table into data/prints.geojson. You can rerun it if the article changes. One location is mine, not Wikipedia’s: print 64, Horikiri Iris Garden , has no coordinates on Wikipedia, so I placed it at today’s Horikiri Iris Garden. The card and the info modal both say so. Hiroshige styling: the Atlas Canvas basemap faded onto washi-paper colour, with a faint grain; a Prussian-blue sky band across the top of the map; season-coloured hanko seals with the print numbers; a red vertical 名所江戸百景 cartouche beside the title; Shippori Mincho for headings. Print cards are fixed-size and show the English, 日本語 and romaji titles, the date, the place and notes. The date is in the old lunisolar calendar, e.g. “Intercalary 5th month, 1857”. Click the image to enlarge it, or follow the link to the full-size scan on Commons. Overlapping prints step through with ‹ › and an “X OF Y” pager; the arrow keys work too. On phones this becomes a bottom sheet with a dimmed backdrop. Search ignores accents, so “ryogoku” finds Ryōgoku. It also accepts Japanese titles and print numbers. There is a lot of functionality and design in this that I did not explicitly request but that is 100% in the spirit of what I wanted. My input time for this simple map was about 15-20 minutes, it helped that the data on the wiki page is so easy to work with. I think the results are pretty good and a few years ago would have been considered great which is an indication of how fast the tech is advancing. The styling is really neat, particularly when you see how little prompt I gave Claude. First attempt at a map of petrol stations in the UK showing fuel prices. Warning this version only has sample data. How does success in elite football correlate with squad cost and revenue? This map shows the patterns using 5 and 10 year indices My first try of a map of all the Grateful Dead’s gigs between 1995 and 2005. Plus album covers, historical notes and archive music. A big project with lots of glitches This is a genuine pub finder map with 50,000 pub points (mainly OSM). It still includes the pub density choropleth from my 1st pub map but the focus has changed. The main challenges were the data pipeline merging OSM and FHRS data and building a fast and intuitive search. Leave a comment Cancel reply Your email address will not be published. Required fields are marked * Comment * Name * Email * Website Enter an answer in digits: nine + 13 = No